<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tarhunt</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tarhun</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tarhunt, also called Tarhun, was the storm god of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> and other Anatolian peoples and one of the chief deities of the Hittite pantheon. The Hittite king ruled as the earthly representative of the storm god rather than as a god himself. This created a more negotiated relationship between royal and priestly authority than existed under Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divine-kingship">divine kingship</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tarhunt.jpg" alt="Tarhunt" width="320" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Verity Cridland · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hetite_God_in_Aleppo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name goes back to a Proto-Anatolian *Tṛḫu-ent-, &quot;conquering&quot;, from an Indo-European root meaning to cross over or overcome, the root of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> trans- and English through. Nearly every Anatolian language used it: Hittite Tarḫunna-, Luwian Tarḫunt-, Carian Trquδ- and Lycian Trqqas. Norbert Oettinger argued that the god's functions come from the Indo-European thunder god *Perkʷūnos, under a new epithet that sounded like the name of the Hattian storm god Taru. The oldest personal name built on it, Tarḫuan, appears in a 19th-century BCE text from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanesh">Kültepe</a>, and the Hittite city of Tarḫuntašša was named after the god.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Among the Luwians the weather god was linked less with the bull than with the horse: a ritual from Arzawa against horse-plague feeds his horses and oils his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a>. His epithets call him powerful, helpful and stern. He ran ahead of the king in battle, ruled the mountains and the heavens, and as the god of the thunderbolt, piḫaššaššiš, gave his name, it is held, to the Greek winged horse Pegasus. The Hittite Great King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muwatalli-ii">Muwatalli II</a> chose the weather god of the thunderbolt as his protector and said the god had raised him and made him king.</p><p>A Luwian innovation was the weather god of the vineyard, first called upon in a 16th-century BCE ritual to make the royal vineyard thrive. In the 8th century BCE King Warpalawas II of Tuwana had him carved on the rock at İvriz holding grapes and ears of wheat. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> was a great city of the weather god from the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>More than sixty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> reliefs and statues show him: bearded and helmeted with axe and thunderbolt, standing on a bull as the weather god of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, or carrying grain and grapes as in Tabal. The bull-borne image returned in Roman times as Jupiter Dolichenus, worshipped from Doliche as far as central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. A relief from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arslantepe">Arslantepe</a> shows him fighting a serpent-like water creature, a myth that may have passed through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> into the Greek story of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> and Typhon.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tarḫunz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Unlike the Egyptian pharaoh, the Hittite king was not a living god but the earthly steward of the storm god <b>Tarhun</b>, and became divine only after death.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Middle and Late Bronze Age Hurrian and Mitannian influence was strong: the Hurrian goddess Hebat was worshipped in Jerusalem, and Baal was held equivalent to the Hurrian storm god Teshub and the Hittite <b>Tarhunt</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/canaanite-religion">Canaanite religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>The storm god <b>Tarhunt</b> fought the serpent Illuyanka, a conflict that resembles that of Indra and Vritra in Vedic mythology and of Thor and Jörmungandr in Norse mythology.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hittite-religion">Hittite religion · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>King Warpalawa ruled with Luwian hieroglyphic inscriptions and is shown on the rock relief at Ivriz before the storm god <b>Tarhunza</b>, who holds grapes and ears of wheat, symbols of the land's fertility.</blockquote><a href="/state/tuwana">Tuwana</a></li></ul></section>
